From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 05:15:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 169/169] fix(gastown): re-key the polecat resume re-verify to
 the step the session holds (gcp-o1nij)

The resume re-verify in the polecat prompt drained every healthy resumed
session, and did so even before gcp-952j9. It started from $GC_BEAD_ID, which
gc exports only to convergence condition scripts and never into a session
shell, so `gc convoy status ""` failed and the block printed
RESUME_INDETERMINATE. Where GC_BEAD_ID was set it then required the WORK bead's
assignee to be this session; since gcp-952j9 nothing assigns the work bead to
the polecat, so it printed OWNERSHIP_LOST. It failed safe (the pool re-serves
the released step) but wasted a session every time.

The block now verifies the STEP this session last claimed. `gc hook current
--id-only` reads the stamp every `gc hook --claim` writes onto the session's own
bead, and that bead survives `gc runtime request-restart`. The step must be
in_progress and assigned to one of the session's five identities, using a copy
of claim_assignee_is_ours restated from the GC_CLAIM block. When GC_BEAD_ID does
name a convoy, the step's molecule must have been poured on it. It never reads
the work bead or metadata.polecat_session, and never writes. A closed step or
no readable claim is RESUME_INDETERMINATE (nothing was taken from this session);
a released step or one held by another session is OWNERSHIP_LOST.

The new suite test_polecat_resume_reverify.sh extracts the block verbatim from
the prompt, runs it against a stubbed gc with a stubbed sleep (no wall-clock
dependence), and pins the two claim_assignee_is_ours copies byte-identical.
Against the old block, the healthy-resume case fails with RESUME_INDETERMINATE
and the unassigned-work-bead case fails with OWNERSHIP_LOST.

Out of scope, kept as today: a session with no current claim drains instead of
re-running the startup claim. No upstream PR (ruling item 11, gcp-bdgg1).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
